1. What is GitHub?
GitHub is an online platform used by developers to store, manage, share, and collaborate on software projects. It works with Git, a version control system that records changes made to files over time. GitHub is commonly used for web development, programming, teamwork, and building a public portfolio.
2. Git vs. GitHub
Git is a version control tool installed on a computer. It tracks changes in a project and allows developers to create commits and branches. GitHub is an online service where Git repositories can be stored and shared with other people.
Git: A version control system.
GitHub: An online platform for hosting and collaborating on Git repositories.
3. Creating a GitHub Account
To use GitHub, create an account with an email address and a password. After signing in, you can create repositories, upload projects, collaborate with others, and manage your code.
4. What is a Repository?
A repository, often called a repo, is a project folder stored on GitHub. It can contain source code, images, documentation, and other project files. A repository also keeps a history of changes made to the project.
5. Important GitHub Terms
Commit: A saved record of changes made to project files.
Push: Sends local commits from your computer to a remote GitHub repository.
Pull: Gets changes from a remote repository and brings them to your local project.
Clone: Creates a local copy of a GitHub repository on your computer.
Branch: A separate line of development used to work on features or changes safely.
Merge: Combines changes from one branch into another branch.
README.md: A documentation file that explains what a project is and how to use it.
6. How to Upload an HTML & CSS Project to GitHub
1. Create your project folder on your computer.
2. Add your HTML file, CSS file, images, and other required files.
3. Open GitHub and sign in to your account.
4. Create a new repository and give it a suitable name.
5. Open the repository and choose the option to add or upload files.
6. Upload your project files.
7. Write a short commit message, such as 'Add HTML and CSS project'.
8. Save the commit. Your project is now stored in the GitHub repository.
7. Example Project Structure
my-website/
│
├── index.html
├── style.css
├── images/
│   └── image.jpg
└── README.md
8. Why is README.md Important?
README.md is usually the first document people see when they open a repository. It explains the project and helps other developers understand its purpose, features, technologies, and how to run or use it.
9. Benefits of GitHub
Stores projects online.
Keeps a history of changes.
Makes teamwork easier.
Allows developers to share their work.
Helps students build a programming portfolio.
Supports open-source projects.
Makes it easier to manage different versions of a project.

10. Conclusion

GitHub is an important platform for students and developers. By learning repositories, commits, pushes, branches, and README files, a beginner can start managing projects and sharing code professionally. For an HTML and CSS student, GitHub is also a useful place to save projects and build a portfolio.

11. Quick Review

Git = version control system.

GitHub = online platform for Git repositories.
Repository = project storage area.
Commit = saved change.


